Date: Fri, 19 Mar 93 12:41:44 +0000
From: Rich Walker <grlw1@cus.cam.ac.uk>
Subject: META: Opportunity for Spontaneous Order

This makes remarkable sense...
The only thing I think it lacks is the addition of

1. regular informing of people how many subscribers there are on the system
or
2. some method of making the monetary values sensible (consider the case
where I post an article (!) with a bid of $1300, and there are 72
subscribers. This means the ExI-Funds should read 18.05555555... If you
want to avoid rounding errors throughtout the system, then

2.1. My ExI-Funds should be passed through unchanged, and I'd put $18 in it.
2.2. My software should only allow me to input a number that's a multiple
of the subscriber base
(but both of these need me to know the number of readers, really...)
2.3. I put up an ExI-Funds value $1300, and the management software notices
that this gives a non-integer value. So it alters my bid to
		int(ExI-Funds / #subscribers) * #subscribers
which is certainly going to go round nicely.

That way, we wouldn't need to worry about funny values.

Of course, if the final bid is going to get munged around by preferences,
there's going to have to be an interesting approach to not losing money. I
suggest this:

since there are always going to be fractions in the system, let them get
lost.  Every so often, total up the amount of money in the system, and find
the shortfall. Then label this as 'taxation', and pass it on to the
appropriate recipient. Has the useful point of being in some manner related
to the usage of the system; indeed, if the code for calculating the
rating-munging is set up to lose fractions throughout, it will probably
produce a tax proportional to
  number of articles posted
  number of preferences recorded for each
  'complexity' of the preference=>money algorithm
and thus will produce a tax that is dependent on the time needed to handle
the money...  

Taking the examples Mark used, message 3528 gets rated 93 by Mark, and the
ExI-Funds bid is $11; Mark is thus offering $21.402. Bob gets $21, and
$.402 disappears in tax.  

Of course, in this case, messages 3526 and 3529 have their cash-back
disappear entirely into tax...  

Well, it's a thought...

