From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Computers in the 3rd World

One big problem is the cost of satellite time for most 3rd
world folks is out of reach, as is laying fiber to the millions 
of villages and burbs.  The connections will go to the universities 
and governments and megacorps, not to the masses, who we would like to 
give the opportunity to bootstrap into the information age.  We
also want to set up direct channels from here to a an extropianizing
3rd world, cutting off the statist monopoly on information flow.

Two pieces of high-tech that work well right now in the 3rd world
are the transistor radio and the pocket calculator.  The calculator
has made running a small 3rd world retail biz much easier, while
the radio gives the farmer access to market prices, weather
reports, etc.

I suggest the following self-transformation kit, mass produced
and distributed throughout the globe:

* portable mini-CD-ROM player ($50) 
* CD-ROMs ($1-$2 each): English/native/Latin/Greek/German/
Japanese dictionary, Oxford dictionary, encyclopedia, Merck manual, 
Physician's Desk Reference, Usenet news and mailing list archives, 
software toolkit (spreadsheet, RDBMS, editor, CAD, etc.), hard-SF 
library (Heinlein, Vinge, Benford, Sterling, etc.), Shakespeare, 
Nietchsze, Extropy, Science, Nature, Economist, university textbooks, 
etc.
* cheap biotech-made vitamins & smart pills
* mini-format (8 mm?) 1st world vids: Teenage Mutant Ninja 
Turtles, Sesame Street, films, Nobel-prize professors'
lectures, etc.

Copyright will dominate the cost of CD-ROM and video wares, but 
as in software we should see massive piracy.  This time not just 
specialized software, but entire First World libraries and university 
curricula may be distributed in millions of copies to every village 
around the globe.

Currently, less than 1/10 of our planet's young people get 
a decent shot at developing their minds, due to lack of
access to the world's info resources.  Less than 1% of the 
world's students get to attend a decent university. Simple, Thai-style
software piracy could bring the planet's best info to everyone 
who wants it by 2010, expanding our planet's extropy dozenfold.


