} involved choose to observe them or because they are imposed by force.

   Richard makes the point well, that there really are no such things
as "rights", except for where we volunteer to observe them... or are 
forced to.  I don't think David would dispute it either.  In fact, I
would have a hard time believing that anyone on this list believes 
that anyone is born with any basic rights.  If someone does, I gladly
challenge them to state where any such rights derive from.

   We all on the other hand seem to agree that property rights have
developed because they are useful in creating motivation.  Motivation
to be self made.  To have your own house, car, library.  The alternative
is something out of Aynn Rand's nightmares... where the parasites who
won't work for their own living, live off those that do.  Most of the
rest of David and Richard's postings continue trying to determine
the advantages and disadvantages of differing forms of ownership.
I on the other hand, would like to spin the conversation back to the
mention of basic rights... 

   Extropianism in general calls for decreasing government intervention
to as little as possible, and furthermore believes that no government
intervention is necessary.  Perry Metzger is the articulate vanguard
or anarcho-capitalism on this list.  Or at least, has come to represent
it to me.  I would be very interested in hearing his responses, or 
indeed the responses from anyone sharing the same mind-frame.

   On what grounds, by what right, do extropians, libertarians, and
anarcho-capitalists call for governments to reduce their control on
markets?  It isn't a question of whether or not the government has 
a right to intervene.  It does intervene.  Does anyone believe that
the government doesn't have a 'right' to intervene?  Regulations and
intervention 'rights' exist because governments force their existence.
Yes, it can logically be reasoned that government intervention is 
not the best use of the available resources.  That government intervention
is bad for the market... but on what grounds should gov't be forced
to give up its control?  What rights do extropians, libertarians,
and anarcho-capitalists have that superseed the governments ability
to enforce its own rights?

   I guess I am just turning this discussion back around to the question
of how anarchists plan on getting the government out of the market.  I
have heard from many, that the market will simply outgrow government.
This sounds reasonable to me... but this posting is specifically for 
those that want anarchy on grounds that it is ideologically superior.
Those that feel strongest that governments are wrong.  Do these people
feel that governments don't have the 'right' to do as they do?  
Personally, I'm not so interested in why they feel governments are 
wrong, as why they feel anarchy is 'right'.

I am not a socialist,

