Date: Fri, 19 Mar 1993 06:36:56 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: reason

Nick:
> 	 Another example of efficient irrationality: Newtonian physics
> 	 is literally false, but still the most widely used physics in
> 	 engineering, due to its simplicity.  

Dean:
> But this is not irrationality.  
> ...Within a restricted domain, it models the
> universe better because it is easier to use to predict things with.

Some have been calling religions "irrational" because they
contain false beliefs, so by that definition Newtonian physics
is irrational, even if only slightly so.  It has _not_
been demonstrated that religious beliefs are less useful, which I take
to mean cause measurably more entropy, except by hearsay (eg "Mormons
who don't stop at stop signs" -- in fact Mormons have above-average
life expectancy).  I challenge all comers to come up with anti-entropic 
benchmarks and use them to compare the entropy of rational vs. irrational 
memes, just as a software engineer might compare the efficiency
and functionality of computer programs.  Let's be rational about
rationality.

Might not efficient simplication also apply to some religious beliefs,
metaphors, poetry, rituals, or teachings like the Ten Commandments?  

Perhaps if we take the voluminous libraries full of psychology, sociology, 
medcine, anthroplogy, sociobiology, nutrition, ethics, etc. data, and 
distill them down to an efficient and extropy-optimizing form, teachable
to the lay person on Sunday mornings, we get something very much like 
Hinduism or Islam or Mormonism or some other memeset that is _prima 
facia_ false but, when examined in detail, reveals a highly evolved 
emergent rationality. Just because their teachings are, like Newtonian 
physics, literally false, does not mean they cannot like Newtonian physics 
be efficiently used.  Given the vastly greater complexity, and corresponding 
less human knowledge about the social world and ethics vs. the 
simplicity and our great knowledge of physics, it should not be
surprising that simple social/ethical models vary much farther from the 
ideal true models, than is the case with simple models in physics.

In no way am I saying that any religion is in an optimal or
ideal form; in fact I suspect that recent radical innovations like
birth control and cryonics have or will render quite a bit
of both contemporary religion and contemporary "rational" ideas
about psychology, sociology, medicine, ethics, etc. obsolete.
We have a great opportunity to borrow from both religious and
rational traditions to form memesets more extropian for the
future; but if we reject memetic structures in whole just
because they are based on false beliefs, we will be left with a 
very parched environment in which to try to grow the new memes.

One rational approach I think has great promise is the study
of human epigenetic motivations, the built-in human meme filters
(such as the alleged "R-site").  A good start on this
is an anthropology/sociobiology text I highly recommned,
_The Evolution of Human Sexuality_, by Donald Symons, which really 
drives to the core of human motivations.  There are many other 
sociobio works that branch into economics, religion, etc. but 
unfortuneately the field is highly underdeveloped.  Most current social
sciences are based on axioms with no more basis in reality than
the faiths of religions (rather they are more academic and high-sounding).
Sociobiology is a school based around the theories in Richard Dawkin's 
_The Selfish Gene_.  It delves into the basic biological motivations 
that academic social scientists have ignored or speculated upon without
data or biologically based theory for so long.

So while I agree that the ideal memeset is entirely rational,
I suspect that a successful, practical lay extropian memeset will 
contain many shortcuts, including false emotion-inducing beliefs that 
motivate extropian behaviors more than true emotionally neutral
beliefs would, and false beliefs that are more efficient than the 
full, complex closest approximations to reality.  We will still
need science out at the forefront, making the most rational models,
and a way to translate those models into extropian rather than
entropian modifications to the world's memesets -- something that
is right now sorely missing.  So I am in no way arguing against
rationality or science, I am rather arguing for a diverse meme
pool that includes both efficient extropianism and full-scale, 
gigalibrary, empirical, cutting-edge rationality.



