Date: Fri, 19 Mar 93 11:07:34 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: MEDIA: Review of Extropy #10

Somebody recently wondered why we don't discuss new issues
of Extropy when they come out.  Good question.  Here's a
review of #10, consisting of random thoughts and comments:

* Overall, Pretty Damn Good.  Best and biggest issue yet.

* Bag the cover.  A picture of Mark Miller?  Yikes!

* Good job with the layout and stuff; quite attractive.

* The photos of article authors are a nice idea, but I think
  they should be a bit smaller -- the size for the 3-column
  pages (e.g. the picture of Hal) seems about right, as opposed
  to two-column size.

* How come the nifty 5-arrow logo doesn't appear anywhere inside?

* Nice job on the circulation!  from 750 to 2500 in one issue!
  There might even be a (gasp) profit soon!

* Pigs in Cyberspace:  Typical Moravec -- mind-expanding and fun.

* Protecting Privacy with Digital Cash: Excellent, thorough piece,
  well-written, informative -- exemplary.

* Technological Self-Transformation: Another great installment
  in Max's series of expansions on the Principles.  My only
  quibble is that the "optimal persona" stuff (which was very
  interesting) had little to do with the topic of the title.

* Miller interview:  The weak point of the issue, for me.  Who
  is this Mark Miller, and why do I care about what he has
  to say?  The interview jumped from marginally-interesting
  topic to marginally-interesting topic... the naive physics
  discussion came across as rather confused; the credit theory
  of identity is rather pedestrian and not very helpful without
  providing some historical context regarding philosophical
  identity theory.  His credentials involve the Xanadu project
  and agoric open systems.  Surely those topics (of great
  interest to Extropians) should at least have been *mentioned*:
  what is the status of the forever-imminent Xanadu project?
  If it's really a failure, why?  How would an agoric open
  system work?  Surely these would be of more interest than
  vague reprises of well-trodden ground regarding consciousness.

* Nanocomputers:  Another really good, solid article; it has
  a weakness, though -- the discussion of the entropy problem
  and reversibility was highly needed (I wish I understood
  the details of this highly unintuitive linkage of information
  theory and thermodynamics -- I still don't believe it...),
  but I couldn't understand the presentation.  If anybody
  has a reference on this, I'd really appreciate it.

* Reviews:  The Objectism review was an interesting essay in itself,
  but seemed to drag on a bit as a book review.  The review of
  Nanosystems was well-written and to the point, but declarations
  that it forms the "keynote publication of the 21st century" and
  comparing Drexler to Galileo are premature at best.  The review
  of Gleick's work on Feynman was perfect.


