Derek Zahn muses:
>The human genome contains 3 x 10^9 base pairs, ...
>30% or so of proteins are involved in the construction of the
>nervous system: 1.3 x 10^7 [or 8] bits ...
>Isn't that interesting?  The "blueprint" for the brain is quite
>simple!  This implies: (1) Interaction with the environment is
>probably important in determining brain structure ("predestined
>learning"), (2) learning in general is overwhelmingly important,
>(3) there are severe limits to hardwired functionality, and (4)
>we have some hope of developing artificial specifications of
>similar complexity.

The number of bits, per se, does not inspire much hope in decoding the
brain.  A cryptographic message with this many bits could be quite
impossible to decode.  Hope comes, I think, from the fact that the
design has to be pretty modular to function under sexual reproduction.
This modularity may allow us to understand one piece at a time.  

Hope also comes from the fact that our high level abilities, those
that distinguish humans from the rest, evolved in relatively few
generations.

These are not, however, enough to pin strong hopes on.

Robin Hanson

