Date: Fri, 19 Mar 93 14:30:21 EST
From: sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu (Mark A. Sulkowski)
Subject: PHIL/POLI: Do rights exist?

Garrett Goebel writes:
>Topic:  Basic Rights are there any?  
>Underlying question:  What right do extropians have to self-governence?
>
>} David Lubkin writes:
>} >Why should it be protected?  For all the usual reasons that any property
>} >is protected.  My basic right to own and control the fruits of my labor.
>}
>Richard Kennaway writes:
>} I think we part company here.  I do not believe in basic rights of any
>} sort.  Rights are social conventions, observed either because those
>} involved choose to observe them or because they are imposed by force.
>
>   Richard makes the point well, that there really are no such things
>as "rights", except for where we volunteer to observe them... or are 
>forced to.  I don't think David would dispute it either.  In fact, I
>would have a hard time believing that anyone on this list believes 
>that anyone is born with any basic rights.  If someone does, I gladly
>challenge them to state where any such rights derive from.

	I think it depends on what you mean by 'rights'.  This is a
deep philosophical question.  I refer you to _Liberty and Nature_ for
the best philosophical defense that I have seen on such issues.

	I think there are at least two senses in which rights 'exist'.
The Declaration of Independence declares that it is the proper role of
government to _secure_ rights.  So, in one sense rights exist as a
result of government action.  Since there are courts and police who
recognize rights (in the ideal government) then their actions aim
at encouraging society to live by those rights.

	Yet if rights are something that are secured, they have an
existence prior to their enforcement, at least as ideas.  Perhaps these
ideas developed as "social conventions".  Customary legal systems may
have had a big role in this.  (See _The Enterprise of Law_ for historical
analysis.)

	The big question is: is there any BASIS for rights?  Are they
purely arbitrary?  Is any possible conception of rights _right_?

	My own view is that rights are a recognition that human beings
have a nature such that they may be harmed or mistreated.  Rights are
rules of behavior, which, if followed, will lead to the minimization
of the harm or mistreatment of others.  Rights, if followed, allow
everyone to live their lives and pursue their own good (happiness,
eudaimonia, whatever you wish to call it).

	Can such rules be violated?  SURE!  Even with government or
PPL such rules can be violated.  Does this mean that rights do not
exist?  Well, even if the idea of rights may not exist in society X,
the BASIS for such rights (the nature of the beings of society X)
still exists.  In THAT sense rights exist and may be violated.


>  It isn't a question of whether or not the government has 
>a right to intervene.  It does intervene.  Does anyone believe that
the government doesn't have a 'right' to intervene?

	Any right that government may claim to intervene is baseless.
It is a misunderstanding of the situation.


>  Regulations and
>intervention 'rights' exist because governments force their existence.

	They exist only in terms of being implemented.  They are not
'right' however.


>Those that feel strongest that governments are wrong.  Do these people
>feel that governments don't have the 'right' to do as they do?  

	Yup.


>Personally, I'm not so interested in why they feel governments are 
>wrong, as why they feel anarchy is 'right'.

	Well, I don't *know* that anarchy is 'right'.  The acid test
is the actual implementation of such a society.  I think that voluntary
interaction is good in the sense that it minimizes the extent to which
people can be forced to be hosts to parasites or to be subjects of
potentially misguided social engineering experiments.  There is much
more I could say, but I am running out of steam.  :)


